k of  redundancy and we're working through  that consultation program right at the  moment that must be pretty heartbreaking  it's devastating and we are losing it's  heartbreaking because we're losing such  good talented people people have made a  massive contribution to this sector and  have a huge amount more to give  and because this isn't just us this is  across the country these people it's  going to be very hard for them to get  jobs again in theater we're going to  lose them from the sector we're going to  lose all the skills and training that  they've had and that really is very hard  to cope with I think part of the reason  that you're seeing theaters now go into  massive redundancy programs is because  the job retention scheme in its current  form isn't going to be fit for purpose  for us anymore when we have zero income  coming in and we have no idea when we're  going to be able to reopen our doors the  idea that we can start paying an  employer contribution from August  through to the end of October just  doesn't work for our sector we can't be  paying more money towards stuff because  we can't be bringing them back to work  and we're not trading and how many staff  do you think you'll be forced to lose  I'd say at least 60 new the sector  employs 300 thousand people across the  country and 70 percent of those jobs are  likely to be at risk talking to you  before the interview you talked about  that gallows humor but this must be a  pretty dark time as well it's very dark  time actually it is yes I think before  before before the interview you caught  my comms manager you know head of comms  and I having a laugh in the corner and  it is it's because it's such a dark time  sometimes the only way that you can cope  with it is a sort of gallows humor but  it's actually not funny it's really not  funny I mean we're staring staring right  down the barrel at the moment of having  no theatre left in this country and I  think people might wake up next April  an